990	# Report language — Simplified Technical English
991	
992	Every sentence a human reads in the report obeys this file. The rules are ASD-STE100
993	(Simplified Technical English), reduced to the part an audit finding needs.
994	
995	## Why
996	
997	A report is read by the developer who must fix the code, often at speed, often not in their
998	first language. A sentence they must decode twice costs the fix, and the bug stays in the
999	code. The scan is only as good as the sentence that delivers it.
1000	
1001	Short words do not make a finding less rigorous. The proof is the diff in the **Fix** block
1002	and the location on the meta line. The sentence exists to say what breaks and who breaks it.
1003	
1004	## What this covers
1005	
1006	Four pieces of text, and no others:
1007	
1008	- the finding **title**
1009	- the **Description** sentence
1010	- the **Lead** description
1011	- the **code smells** list on a Lead
1012	
1013	## What it must never touch
1014	
1015	These are data, not prose. Rewriting one of them for readability breaks the scan:
1016	
1017	- **Solidity identifiers** — contract, function, variable and event names. They are printed as
1018	  the source spells them. `withdrawToNativeChain` is never softened to `withdraw to chain`.
1019	- **The diff inside a Fix block.** It is code. It is pasted verbatim from the agent that wrote
1020	  it, and `judging.md` and `dedup-and-assembly.md` both forbid paraphrase there.
1021	- **The bug-class label** (`zero-abort-address`). It is the third segment of a memory key.
1022	  The same bug under a plainer word is a second ledger record, so memory remembers it twice
1023	  and recognises it never. Labels are chosen by the rules in `dedup-and-assembly.md` step 1,
1024	  never by this file.
1025	- **Fixed strings the assembler prints** — the banner, the Scope table headers, the italic
1026	  lines under Leads and "Known from earlier scans", the disclaimer. They are in
1027	  `assemble.sh` and they are already written.
1028	
1029	## The rules
1030	
1031	1. **One sentence, one idea.** A Description is one sentence. A Lead description is one or
1032	   two. Never three.
1033	2. **Twenty-five words is the ceiling.** Count them. Over the ceiling means two ideas in one
1034	   sentence — cut it or split it.
1035	3. **Active voice, and name the actor.** Who does this? Write that word first.
1036	   - No: `Funds can be drained due to a missing check.`
1037	   - Yes: `Any caller can take all the tokens, because the function checks no caller.`
1038	4. **Simple present tense.** The code does this today. Not `would be able to`, not
1039	   `could potentially`. If the path is uncertain it is a Lead, and the Lead says which step
1040	   is unproven.
1041	5. **No `-ing` clauses.** They hide the actor and the order of events. Use `that`, `so`, or a
1042	   second sentence.
1043	   - No: `Missing access control allowing anyone to drain the vault.`
1044	   - Yes: `The function has no access control, so any caller can take all the tokens.`
1045	6. **Keep the articles.** `the`, `a`, `an`. Telegraphic style — `Function reverts on zero
1046	   amount` — is not shorter to read, only shorter to type.
1047	7. **Three nouns in a row is the limit.** Break a longer stack with `of`, `for` or `in`.
1048	   - No: `cross-chain message replay protection gap`
1049	   - Yes: `the contract does not stop a replay of a cross-chain message`
1050	8. **One word for one thing, every time.** Pick `token`, or pick `asset`, and use that word
1051	   in the title, the Description and the fix. A synonym reads as a second thing.
1052	9. **No metaphor, no idiom, no slang.** See the table below. A metaphor is a word the reader
1053	   must translate before they can act.
1054	10. **Say what happens, not how bad it is.** `catastrophic`, `critical`, `severe` and
1055	    `trivially` carry no information — the confidence number and the Fix block carry it. Write
1056	    the effect: `the caller keeps the tokens and the pool keeps the debt`.
1057	11. **Positive statements. No double negatives.** `The check is absent` beats `the check is
1058	    not present`.
1059	12. **Event order is sentence order.** Cause, then effect. `The price comes from the pool
1060	    balance, so an attacker who moves the balance moves the price.`
1061	13. **A title is one clause, twelve words or fewer,** present tense, active, and it names the
1062	    defect or its effect. `Swap trusts a spot price as an oracle.`
1063	    Not `Oracle manipulation vulnerability in swap functionality`.
1064	
1065	## Words to replace
1066	
1067	Left column: the wording to keep out of the report. Right column: what the report prints.
1068	
1069	| Do not write | Write |
1070	| --- | --- |
1071	| burns the funds | sends the tokens to an address that cannot spend them |
1072	| bricks the contract | makes the function fail for every caller, forever |
1073	| rug / rugpull | the owner takes the user deposits |
1074	| griefing | an attacker makes the function fail for other users |
1075	| footgun | (delete the word and name the defect) |
1076	| silently swallows the error | ignores the error |
1077	| DoS / denial of service | blocks every caller |
1078	| attack surface / attack vector | path |
1079	| malicious actor / bad actor | an attacker |
1080	| leverages / utilizes | uses |
1081	| is able to | can |
1082	| in order to | to |
1083	| arbitrary value | any value the caller chooses |
1084	| stale price | old price |
1085	| sanity check | check |
1086	| happy path | the normal path |
1087	| atomically | in one transaction |
1088	| trustlessly / permissionlessly | (delete, or name who may call) |
1089	| non-zero | more than zero |
1090	| cascading failures | (name each failure) |
1091	| exponentially / trivially / catastrophically | (delete) |
1092	| edge case | (name the input or the state) |
1093	
1094	## Technical names stay
1095	
1096	STE-100 keeps technical names, and a security report cannot work without them:
1097	`reentrancy`, `slippage`, `oracle`, `revert`, `overflow`, `rounding`, `allowance`, `nonce`,
1098	`slot`, `delegatecall`. Use them. They are precise and the reader who fixes Solidity knows
1099	them.
1100	
1101	The rule is what goes **around** them: name the mechanism once with its technical name, then
1102	say in plain words what the attacker gets.
1103	
1104	- No: `Classic CEI violation enabling reentrant drainage of the vault.`
1105	- Yes: `The function sends ETH before it lowers the balance, so a reentrant call withdraws
1106	  the same deposit twice.`
1107	
1108	## Worked examples
1109	
1110	Before, and after. The code the sentence describes did not change.
1111	
1112	**One**
1113	
1114	- Before: `Improper validation of the abort address results in a catastrophic loss of user
1115	  funds via burning to the zero address.`
1116	- After: `The function accepts a zero abort address, so a failed transfer sends the tokens to
1117	  an address that nobody controls.`
1118	
1119	**Two**
1120	
1121	- Before: `Lack of slippage protection in the swap path exposes users to sandwich attacks,
1122	  potentially leading to significant value extraction by MEV searchers.`
1123	- After: `The swap sets the minimum output to zero, so an attacker who trades before and
1124	  after the victim keeps most of the output.`
1125	
1126	**Three — a Lead**
1127	
1128	- Before: `Potential accounting inconsistency stemming from asymmetric fee handling on
1129	  deposit/withdraw flows warranting further investigation.`
1130	- After: `The deposit takes the fee from the amount, and the withdrawal takes it from the
1131	  balance. We did not find the input that makes the two disagree.`
1132	
1133	## Self-check before you write the block
1134	
1135	Four questions. Any `no` means rewrite the sentence, not the code:
1136	
1137	- Does the sentence name **who** acts and **what** they get?
1138	- Is it one sentence, twenty-five words or fewer, with no `-ing` clause?
1139	- Is every word in it a word the reader does not have to translate?
1140	- Are the identifiers, the label and the diff exactly as the source and the agent wrote them?
