974	concrete is already plain.
975	# Report language — Simplified Technical English
976	
977	Every sentence a human reads in the report obeys this file. The rules are ASD-STE100
978	(Simplified Technical English), reduced to the part an audit finding needs.
979	
980	## Why
981	
982	A report is read by the developer who must fix the code, often at speed, often not in their
983	first language. A sentence they must decode twice costs the fix, and the bug stays in the
984	code. The scan is only as good as the sentence that delivers it.
985	
986	Short words do not make a finding less rigorous. The proof is the diff in the **Fix** block
987	and the location on the meta line. The sentence exists to say what breaks and who breaks it.
988	
989	## What this covers
990	
991	Four pieces of text, and no others:
992	
993	- the finding **title**
994	- the **Description** sentence
995	- the **Lead** description
996	- the **code smells** list on a Lead
997	
998	## What it must never touch
999	
1000	These are data, not prose. Rewriting one of them for readability breaks the scan:
1001	
1002	- **Solidity identifiers** — contract, function, variable and event names. They are printed as
1003	  the source spells them. `withdrawToNativeChain` is never softened to `withdraw to chain`.
1004	- **The diff inside a Fix block.** It is code. It is pasted verbatim from the agent that wrote
1005	  it, and `judging.md` and `dedup-and-assembly.md` both forbid paraphrase there.
1006	- **The bug-class label** (`zero-abort-address`). It is the third segment of a memory key.
1007	  The same bug under a plainer word is a second ledger record, so memory remembers it twice
1008	  and recognises it never. Labels are chosen by the rules in `dedup-and-assembly.md` step 1,
1009	  never by this file.
1010	- **Fixed strings the assembler prints** — the banner, the Scope table headers, the italic
1011	  lines under Leads and "Known from earlier scans", the disclaimer. They are in
1012	  `assemble.sh` and they are already written.
1013	
1014	## The rules
1015	
1016	1. **One sentence, one idea.** A Description is one sentence. A Lead description is one or
1017	   two. Never three.
1018	2. **Twenty-five words is the ceiling.** Count them. Over the ceiling means two ideas in one
1019	   sentence — cut it or split it.
1020	3. **Active voice, and name the actor.** Who does this? Write that word first.
1021	   - No: `Funds can be drained due to a missing check.`
1022	   - Yes: `Any caller can take all the tokens, because the function checks no caller.`
1023	4. **Simple present tense.** The code does this today. Not `would be able to`, not
1024	   `could potentially`. If the path is uncertain it is a Lead, and the Lead says which step
1025	   is unproven.
1026	5. **No `-ing` clauses.** They hide the actor and the order of events. Use `that`, `so`, or a
1027	   second sentence.
1028	   - No: `Missing access control allowing anyone to drain the vault.`
1029	   - Yes: `The function has no access control, so any caller can take all the tokens.`
1030	6. **Keep the articles.** `the`, `a`, `an`. Telegraphic style — `Function reverts on zero
1031	   amount` — is not shorter to read, only shorter to type.
1032	7. **Three nouns in a row is the limit.** Break a longer stack with `of`, `for` or `in`.
1033	   - No: `cross-chain message replay protection gap`
1034	   - Yes: `the contract does not stop a replay of a cross-chain message`
1035	8. **One word for one thing, every time.** Pick `token`, or pick `asset`, and use that word
1036	   in the title, the Description and the fix. A synonym reads as a second thing.
1037	9. **No metaphor, no idiom, no slang.** See the table below. A metaphor is a word the reader
1038	   must translate before they can act.
1039	10. **Say what happens, not how bad it is.** `catastrophic`, `critical`, `severe` and
1040	    `trivially` carry no information — the confidence number and the Fix block carry it. Write
1041	    the effect: `the caller keeps the tokens and the pool keeps the debt`.
1042	11. **Positive statements. No double negatives.** `The check is absent` beats `the check is
1043	    not present`.
1044	12. **Event order is sentence order.** Cause, then effect. `The price comes from the pool
1045	    balance, so an attacker who moves the balance moves the price.`
1046	13. **A title is one clause, twelve words or fewer,** present tense, active, and it names the
1047	    defect or its effect. `Swap trusts a spot price as an oracle.`
1048	    Not `Oracle manipulation vulnerability in swap functionality`.
1049	
1050	## Words to replace
1051	
1052	Left column: the wording to keep out of the report. Right column: what the report prints.
1053	
1054	| Do not write | Write |
1055	| --- | --- |
1056	| burns the funds | sends the tokens to an address that cannot spend them |
1057	| bricks the contract | makes the function fail for every caller, forever |
1058	| rug / rugpull | the owner takes the user deposits |
1059	| griefing | an attacker makes the function fail for other users |
1060	| footgun | (delete the word and name the defect) |
1061	| silently swallows the error | ignores the error |
1062	| DoS / denial of service | blocks every caller |
1063	| attack surface / attack vector | path |
1064	| malicious actor / bad actor | an attacker |
1065	| leverages / utilizes | uses |
1066	| is able to | can |
1067	| in order to | to |
1068	| arbitrary value | any value the caller chooses |
1069	| stale price | old price |
1070	| sanity check | check |
1071	| happy path | the normal path |
1072	| atomically | in one transaction |
1073	| trustlessly / permissionlessly | (delete, or name who may call) |
1074	| non-zero | more than zero |
1075	| cascading failures | (name each failure) |
1076	| exponentially / trivially / catastrophically | (delete) |
1077	| edge case | (name the input or the state) |
