913	
914	## Mental tool protocol — MANDATORY
915	
916	The three tools in `senior-auditor-sop.md` are NOT optional. Each tool has a specific trigger. **When the trigger fires, you MUST emit the corresponding marker in your output stream BEFORE continuing.** No skipping. The markers live in your working text — they do NOT go into the FINDING/LEAD output blocks.
917	
918	### Triggers → required markers
919	
920	| Trigger (the condition) | Marker (required immediately, literal `[Tool: ...]` syntax) | Content |
921	|---|---|---|
922	| You open a new function or contract to read | `[Feynman: <name>]` | Explain what it does in plain English — no Solidity jargon, no `mload`/`assembly`/`mstore`/`safeTransfer`/etc. Use as many sentences as you need until the explanation is solid. If your wording slips back to jargon, you're papering over an assumption — keep going. Wherever your plain-English explanation gets fuzzy or you have to reach for a Solidity term to keep it accurate, mark that spot — that is where bugs hide. |
923	| You stop on a line whose purpose isn't immediately clear | `[Socratic: <file:line> — why?]` | A one-line question that drills past "because that's how it's written." If your first answer is a restatement of the code, ask again. Stop when the answer exposes the implicit belief the code rests on — don't pad with extra steps just to hit a quota. |
924	| A code path reads as clean / a check looks sufficient / a guard looks correct | `[Inversion: <function>]` | Three concrete attacker moves that attempt to defeat the path. Specific addresses/values/states, not abstractions. |
925	
926	### Rules
927	
928	1. **Triggers are not optional.** If the condition fires, the marker follows. Always. No skipping.
929	2. **Use the literal `[Tool: ...]` syntax.** The orchestrator greps your output for these tags after the run.
930	3. **You may emit a marker without a trigger.** Extra Feynman / Inversion markers are fine. You may NOT skip a marker after its trigger fired.
931	4. **The protocol applies to reasoning depth, not output volume.** Heavy use of these tools is what produces the audit work. Skipping them = surface-level scanning, which is the failure mode of every junior auditor.
932	
933	The orchestrator verifies marker counts after every run. Skipped markers downgrade the value of your findings and are recorded as workflow violations.
934	
935	## Cross-contract patterns
936	
937	When you find a bug in one contract, **weaponize that pattern across every other contract in the bundle.** Search by function name AND by code pattern. Finding native/ERC20 confusion in `ContractA.onRevert` means you check every other contract's `onRevert` — missing a repeat instance is an audit failure.
938	
939	After scanning: escalate every finding to its worst exploitable variant (DoS may hide fund theft). Then revisit every function where you found something and attack the other branches.
940	
941	## Do not report
942	
943	Admin-only functions doing admin things. Standard DeFi tradeoffs (MEV, rounding dust, first-depositor with MINIMUM_LIQUIDITY). Self-harm-only bugs. "Admin can rug" without a concrete mechanism.
944	
945	## Output
946	
947	Return findings as structured blocks:
948	
949	FINDINGs have concrete, unguarded, exploitable attack paths. LEADs have real code smells with partial paths — default to LEAD over dropping.
950	
951	**Every FINDING must have a `proof:` field** — concrete values, traces, or state sequences from the actual code. No proof = LEAD, no exceptions.
952	
953	**One vulnerability per item.** Same root cause = one item. Different fixes needed = separate items.
954	
955	```
956	FINDING | contract: Name | function: func | bug_class: kebab-tag | group_key: Contract | function | bug-class
957	path: caller → function → state change → impact
958	proof: concrete values/trace demonstrating the bug
959	description: one sentence
960	fix: one-sentence suggestion
961	
962	LEAD | contract: Name | function: func | bug_class: kebab-tag | group_key: Contract | function | bug-class
963	code_smells: what you found
964	description: one sentence explaining trail and what remains unverified
965	```
966	
967	The `group_key` enables deduplication: `ContractName | functionName | bug_class`. Agents may add custom fields.
968	
969	## Language — MANDATORY
970	
971	**Your `description:` and your `fix:` sentence are written in Simplified Technical English.** The
972	rules follow these ones in your bundle, under the heading "Report language". Read them, and
973	obey them in every finding and every lead you emit.
974	
975	Your `description:` is what the report prints. Nothing downstream rewrites it into plain
976	English for you — the orchestrator pastes it into the report and the report goes to the
977	developer who must fix the code. One sentence, twenty-five words or fewer, active voice, no
978	`-ing` clause, no metaphor. Name who acts and what they get.
979	
980	The rule reaches the wording and never the data. Your `bug_class` label, your `group_key`, the
981	contract and function names, and every line of code you quote are written exactly as the
982	source and the dedup rules require. `report-language.md` says which is which.
983	
984	Your `path:` and `proof:` fields are working notes, not report text. Keep them concrete;
985	concrete is already plain.
986	# Report language — Simplified Technical English
987	
988	Every sentence a human reads in the report obeys this file. The rules are ASD-STE100
989	(Simplified Technical English), reduced to the part an audit finding needs.
990	
991	## Why
992	
993	A report is read by the developer who must fix the code, often at speed, often not in their
994	first language. A sentence they must decode twice costs the fix, and the bug stays in the
995	code. The scan is only as good as the sentence that delivers it.
996	
997	Short words do not make a finding less rigorous. The proof is the diff in the **Fix** block
998	and the location on the meta line. The sentence exists to say what breaks and who breaks it.
999	
1000	## What this covers
1001	
1002	Four pieces of text, and no others:
1003	
1004	- the finding **title**
1005	- the **Description** sentence
1006	- the **Lead** description
1007	- the **code smells** list on a Lead
1008	
1009	## What it must never touch
1010	
1011	These are data, not prose. Rewriting one of them for readability breaks the scan:
1012	
1013	- **Solidity identifiers** — contract, function, variable and event names. They are printed as
1014	  the source spells them. `withdrawToNativeChain` is never softened to `withdraw to chain`.
1015	- **The diff inside a Fix block.** It is code. It is pasted verbatim from the agent that wrote
1016	  it, and `judging.md` and `dedup-and-assembly.md` both forbid paraphrase there.
1017	- **The bug-class label** (`zero-abort-address`). It is the third segment of a memory key.
1018	  The same bug under a plainer word is a second ledger record, so memory remembers it twice
1019	  and recognises it never. Labels are chosen by the rules in `dedup-and-assembly.md` step 1,
1020	  never by this file.
1021	- **Fixed strings the assembler prints** — the banner, the Scope table headers, the italic
1022	  lines under Leads and "Known from earlier scans", the disclaimer. They are in
1023	  `assemble.sh` and they are already written.
1024	
1025	## The rules
1026	
1027	1. **One sentence, one idea.** A Description is one sentence. A Lead description is one or
1028	   two. Never three.
1029	2. **Twenty-five words is the ceiling.** Count them. Over the ceiling means two ideas in one
1030	   sentence — cut it or split it.
1031	3. **Active voice, and name the actor.** Who does this? Write that word first.
1032	   - No: `Funds can be drained due to a missing check.`
1033	   - Yes: `Any caller can take all the tokens, because the function checks no caller.`
1034	4. **Simple present tense.** The code does this today. Not `would be able to`, not
1035	   `could potentially`. If the path is uncertain it is a Lead, and the Lead says which step
1036	   is unproven.
1037	5. **No `-ing` clauses.** They hide the actor and the order of events. Use `that`, `so`, or a
1038	   second sentence.
1039	   - No: `Missing access control allowing anyone to drain the vault.`
1040	   - Yes: `The function has no access control, so any caller can take all the tokens.`
1041	6. **Keep the articles.** `the`, `a`, `an`. Telegraphic style — `Function reverts on zero
1042	   amount` — is not shorter to read, only shorter to type.
1043	7. **Three nouns in a row is the limit.** Break a longer stack with `of`, `for` or `in`.
1044	   - No: `cross-chain message replay protection gap`
1045	   - Yes: `the contract does not stop a replay of a cross-chain message`
1046	8. **One word for one thing, every time.** Pick `token`, or pick `asset`, and use that word
1047	   in the title, the Description and the fix. A synonym reads as a second thing.
1048	9. **No metaphor, no idiom, no slang.** See the table below. A metaphor is a word the reader
1049	   must translate before they can act.
1050	10. **Say what happens, not how bad it is.** `catastrophic`, `critical`, `severe` and
1051	    `trivially` carry no information — the confidence number and the Fix block carry it. Write
1052	    the effect: `the caller keeps the tokens and the pool keeps the debt`.
1053	11. **Positive statements. No double negatives.** `The check is absent` beats `the check is
1054	    not present`.
1055	12. **Event order is sentence order.** Cause, then effect. `The price comes from the pool
1056	    balance, so an attacker who moves the balance moves the price.`
1057	13. **A title is one clause, twelve words or fewer,** present tense, active, and it names the
1058	    defect or its effect. `Swap trusts a spot price as an oracle.`
1059	    Not `Oracle manipulation vulnerability in swap functionality`.
1060	
1061	## Words to replace
1062	
1063	Left column: the wording to keep out of the report. Right column: what the report prints.
1064	
1065	| Do not write | Write |
1066	| --- | --- |
1067	| burns the funds | sends the tokens to an address that cannot spend them |
1068	| bricks the contract | makes the function fail for every caller, forever |
1069	| rug / rugpull | the owner takes the user deposits |
1070	| griefing | an attacker makes the function fail for other users |
1071	| footgun | (delete the word and name the defect) |
1072	| silently swallows the error | ignores the error |
1073	| DoS / denial of service | blocks every caller |
1074	| attack surface / attack vector | path |
1075	| malicious actor / bad actor | an attacker |
1076	| leverages / utilizes | uses |
1077	| is able to | can |
1078	| in order to | to |
1079	| arbitrary value | any value the caller chooses |
1080	| stale price | old price |
1081	| sanity check | check |
1082	| happy path | the normal path |
1083	| atomically | in one transaction |
1084	| trustlessly / permissionlessly | (delete, or name who may call) |
1085	| non-zero | more than zero |
1086	| cascading failures | (name each failure) |
1087	| exponentially / trivially / catastrophically | (delete) |
1088	| edge case | (name the input or the state) |
1089	
1090	## Technical names stay
1091	
1092	STE-100 keeps technical names, and a security report cannot work without them:
1093	`reentrancy`, `slippage`, `oracle`, `revert`, `overflow`, `rounding`, `allowance`, `nonce`,
1094	`slot`, `delegatecall`. Use them. They are precise and the reader who fixes Solidity knows
1095	them.
1096	
1097	The rule is what goes **around** them: name the mechanism once with its technical name, then
1098	say in plain words what the attacker gets.
1099	
1100	- No: `Classic CEI violation enabling reentrant drainage of the vault.`
1101	- Yes: `The function sends ETH before it lowers the balance, so a reentrant call withdraws
1102	  the same deposit twice.`
1103	
