1019	# Report language — Simplified Technical English
1020	
1021	Every sentence a human reads in the report obeys this file. The rules are ASD-STE100
1022	(Simplified Technical English), reduced to the part an audit finding needs.
1023	
1024	## Why
1025	
1026	A report is read by the developer who must fix the code, often at speed, often not in their
1027	first language. A sentence they must decode twice costs the fix, and the bug stays in the
1028	code. The scan is only as good as the sentence that delivers it.
1029	
1030	Short words do not make a finding less rigorous. The proof is the diff in the **Fix** block
1031	and the location on the meta line. The sentence exists to say what breaks and who breaks it.
1032	
1033	## What this covers
1034	
1035	Four pieces of text, and no others:
1036	
1037	- the finding **title**
1038	- the **Description** sentence
1039	- the **Lead** description
1040	- the **code smells** list on a Lead
1041	
1042	## What it must never touch
1043	
1044	These are data, not prose. Rewriting one of them for readability breaks the scan:
1045	
1046	- **Solidity identifiers** — contract, function, variable and event names. They are printed as
1047	  the source spells them. `withdrawToNativeChain` is never softened to `withdraw to chain`.
1048	- **The diff inside a Fix block.** It is code. It is pasted verbatim from the agent that wrote
1049	  it, and `judging.md` and `dedup-and-assembly.md` both forbid paraphrase there.
1050	- **The bug-class label** (`zero-abort-address`). It is the third segment of a memory key.
1051	  The same bug under a plainer word is a second ledger record, so memory remembers it twice
1052	  and recognises it never. Labels are chosen by the rules in `dedup-and-assembly.md` step 1,
1053	  never by this file.
1054	- **Fixed strings the assembler prints** — the banner, the Scope table headers, the italic
1055	  lines under Leads and "Known from earlier scans", the disclaimer. They are in
1056	  `assemble.sh` and they are already written.
1057	
1058	## The rules
1059	
1060	1. **One sentence, one idea.** A Description is one sentence. A Lead description is one or
1061	   two. Never three.
1062	2. **Twenty-five words is the ceiling.** Count them. Over the ceiling means two ideas in one
1063	   sentence — cut it or split it.
1064	3. **Active voice, and name the actor.** Who does this? Write that word first.
1065	   - No: `Funds can be drained due to a missing check.`
1066	   - Yes: `Any caller can take all the tokens, because the function checks no caller.`
1067	4. **Simple present tense.** The code does this today. Not `would be able to`, not
1068	   `could potentially`. If the path is uncertain it is a Lead, and the Lead says which step
1069	   is unproven.
1070	5. **No `-ing` clauses.** They hide the actor and the order of events. Use `that`, `so`, or a
1071	   second sentence.
1072	   - No: `Missing access control allowing anyone to drain the vault.`
1073	   - Yes: `The function has no access control, so any caller can take all the tokens.`
1074	6. **Keep the articles.** `the`, `a`, `an`. Telegraphic style — `Function reverts on zero
1075	   amount` — is not shorter to read, only shorter to type.
1076	7. **Three nouns in a row is the limit.** Break a longer stack with `of`, `for` or `in`.
1077	   - No: `cross-chain message replay protection gap`
1078	   - Yes: `the contract does not stop a replay of a cross-chain message`
1079	8. **One word for one thing, every time.** Pick `token`, or pick `asset`, and use that word
1080	   in the title, the Description and the fix. A synonym reads as a second thing.
1081	9. **No metaphor, no idiom, no slang.** See the table below. A metaphor is a word the reader
1082	   must translate before they can act.
1083	10. **Say what happens, not how bad it is.** `catastrophic`, `critical`, `severe` and
1084	    `trivially` carry no information — the confidence number and the Fix block carry it. Write
1085	    the effect: `the caller keeps the tokens and the pool keeps the debt`.
1086	11. **Positive statements. No double negatives.** `The check is absent` beats `the check is
1087	    not present`.
1088	12. **Event order is sentence order.** Cause, then effect. `The price comes from the pool
1089	    balance, so an attacker who moves the balance moves the price.`
1090	13. **A title is one clause, twelve words or fewer,** present tense, active, and it names the
1091	    defect or its effect. `Swap trusts a spot price as an oracle.`
1092	    Not `Oracle manipulation vulnerability in swap functionality`.
1093	
1094	## Words to replace
1095	
1096	Left column: the wording to keep out of the report. Right column: what the report prints.
1097	
1098	| Do not write | Write |
1099	| --- | --- |
1100	| burns the funds | sends the tokens to an address that cannot spend them |
1101	| bricks the contract | makes the function fail for every caller, forever |
1102	| rug / rugpull | the owner takes the user deposits |
1103	| griefing | an attacker makes the function fail for other users |
1104	| footgun | (delete the word and name the defect) |
1105	| silently swallows the error | ignores the error |
1106	| DoS / denial of service | blocks every caller |
1107	| attack surface / attack vector | path |
1108	| malicious actor / bad actor | an attacker |
1109	| leverages / utilizes | uses |
1110	| is able to | can |
1111	| in order to | to |
1112	| arbitrary value | any value the caller chooses |
1113	| stale price | old price |
1114	| sanity check | check |
1115	| happy path | the normal path |
1116	| atomically | in one transaction |
1117	| trustlessly / permissionlessly | (delete, or name who may call) |
1118	| non-zero | more than zero |
1119	| cascading failures | (name each failure) |
1120	| exponentially / trivially / catastrophically | (delete) |
1121	| edge case | (name the input or the state) |
1122	
1123	## Technical names stay
1124	
1125	STE-100 keeps technical names, and a security report cannot work without them:
1126	`reentrancy`, `slippage`, `oracle`, `revert`, `overflow`, `rounding`, `allowance`, `nonce`,
1127	`slot`, `delegatecall`. Use them. They are precise and the reader who fixes Solidity knows
1128	them.
1129	
1130	The rule is what goes **around** them: name the mechanism once with its technical name, then
1131	say in plain words what the attacker gets.
1132	
1133	- No: `Classic CEI violation enabling reentrant drainage of the vault.`
1134	- Yes: `The function sends ETH before it lowers the balance, so a reentrant call withdraws
1135	  the same deposit twice.`
1136	
1137	## Worked examples
1138	
1139	Before, and after. The code the sentence describes did not change.
1140	
1141	**One**
1142	
1143	- Before: `Improper validation of the abort address results in a catastrophic loss of user
1144	  funds via burning to the zero address.`
1145	- After: `The function accepts a zero abort address, so a failed transfer sends the tokens to
1146	  an address that nobody controls.`
1147	
1148	**Two**
1149	
1150	- Before: `Lack of slippage protection in the swap path exposes users to sandwich attacks,
1151	  potentially leading to significant value extraction by MEV searchers.`
1152	- After: `The swap sets the minimum output to zero, so an attacker who trades before and
1153	  after the victim keeps most of the output.`
1154	
1155	**Three — a Lead**
1156	
1157	- Before: `Potential accounting inconsistency stemming from asymmetric fee handling on
1158	  deposit/withdraw flows warranting further investigation.`
1159	- After: `The deposit takes the fee from the amount, and the withdrawal takes it from the
1160	  balance. We did not find the input that makes the two disagree.`
1161	
1162	## Self-check before you write the block
1163	
1164	Four questions. Any `no` means rewrite the sentence, not the code:
1165	
1166	- Does the sentence name **who** acts and **what** they get?
1167	- Is it one sentence, twenty-five words or fewer, with no `-ing` clause?
1168	- Is every word in it a word the reader does not have to translate?
1169	- Are the identifiers, the label and the diff exactly as the source and the agent wrote them?
