1021	# Report language — Simplified Technical English
1022	
1023	Every sentence a human reads in the report obeys this file. The rules are ASD-STE100
1024	(Simplified Technical English), reduced to the part an audit finding needs.
1025	
1026	## Why
1027	
1028	A report is read by the developer who must fix the code, often at speed, often not in their
1029	first language. A sentence they must decode twice costs the fix, and the bug stays in the
1030	code. The scan is only as good as the sentence that delivers it.
1031	
1032	Short words do not make a finding less rigorous. The proof is the diff in the **Fix** block
1033	and the location on the meta line. The sentence exists to say what breaks and who breaks it.
1034	
1035	## What this covers
1036	
1037	Four pieces of text, and no others:
1038	
1039	- the finding **title**
1040	- the **Description** sentence
1041	- the **Lead** description
1042	- the **code smells** list on a Lead
1043	
1044	## What it must never touch
1045	
1046	These are data, not prose. Rewriting one of them for readability breaks the scan:
1047	
1048	- **Solidity identifiers** — contract, function, variable and event names. They are printed as
1049	  the source spells them. `withdrawToNativeChain` is never softened to `withdraw to chain`.
1050	- **The diff inside a Fix block.** It is code. It is pasted verbatim from the agent that wrote
1051	  it, and `judging.md` and `dedup-and-assembly.md` both forbid paraphrase there.
1052	- **The bug-class label** (`zero-abort-address`). It is the third segment of a memory key.
1053	  The same bug under a plainer word is a second ledger record, so memory remembers it twice
1054	  and recognises it never. Labels are chosen by the rules in `dedup-and-assembly.md` step 1,
1055	  never by this file.
1056	- **Fixed strings the assembler prints** — the banner, the Scope table headers, the italic
1057	  lines under Leads and "Known from earlier scans", the disclaimer. They are in
1058	  `assemble.sh` and they are already written.
1059	
1060	## The rules
1061	
1062	1. **One sentence, one idea.** A Description is one sentence. A Lead description is one or
1063	   two. Never three.
1064	2. **Twenty-five words is the ceiling.** Count them. Over the ceiling means two ideas in one
1065	   sentence — cut it or split it.
1066	3. **Active voice, and name the actor.** Who does this? Write that word first.
1067	   - No: `Funds can be drained due to a missing check.`
1068	   - Yes: `Any caller can take all the tokens, because the function checks no caller.`
1069	4. **Simple present tense.** The code does this today. Not `would be able to`, not
1070	   `could potentially`. If the path is uncertain it is a Lead, and the Lead says which step
1071	   is unproven.
1072	5. **No `-ing` clauses.** They hide the actor and the order of events. Use `that`, `so`, or a
1073	   second sentence.
1074	   - No: `Missing access control allowing anyone to drain the vault.`
1075	   - Yes: `The function has no access control, so any caller can take all the tokens.`
1076	6. **Keep the articles.** `the`, `a`, `an`. Telegraphic style — `Function reverts on zero
1077	   amount` — is not shorter to read, only shorter to type.
1078	7. **Three nouns in a row is the limit.** Break a longer stack with `of`, `for` or `in`.
1079	   - No: `cross-chain message replay protection gap`
1080	   - Yes: `the contract does not stop a replay of a cross-chain message`
1081	8. **One word for one thing, every time.** Pick `token`, or pick `asset`, and use that word
1082	   in the title, the Description and the fix. A synonym reads as a second thing.
1083	9. **No metaphor, no idiom, no slang.** See the table below. A metaphor is a word the reader
1084	   must translate before they can act.
1085	10. **Say what happens, not how bad it is.** `catastrophic`, `critical`, `severe` and
1086	    `trivially` carry no information — the confidence number and the Fix block carry it. Write
1087	    the effect: `the caller keeps the tokens and the pool keeps the debt`.
1088	11. **Positive statements. No double negatives.** `The check is absent` beats `the check is
1089	    not present`.
1090	12. **Event order is sentence order.** Cause, then effect. `The price comes from the pool
1091	    balance, so an attacker who moves the balance moves the price.`
1092	13. **A title is one clause, twelve words or fewer,** present tense, active, and it names the
1093	    defect or its effect. `Swap trusts a spot price as an oracle.`
1094	    Not `Oracle manipulation vulnerability in swap functionality`.
1095	
1096	## Words to replace
1097	
1098	Left column: the wording to keep out of the report. Right column: what the report prints.
1099	
1100	| Do not write | Write |
1101	| --- | --- |
1102	| burns the funds | sends the tokens to an address that cannot spend them |
1103	| bricks the contract | makes the function fail for every caller, forever |
1104	| rug / rugpull | the owner takes the user deposits |
1105	| griefing | an attacker makes the function fail for other users |
1106	| footgun | (delete the word and name the defect) |
1107	| silently swallows the error | ignores the error |
1108	| DoS / denial of service | blocks every caller |
1109	| attack surface / attack vector | path |
1110	| malicious actor / bad actor | an attacker |
1111	| leverages / utilizes | uses |
1112	| is able to | can |
1113	| in order to | to |
1114	| arbitrary value | any value the caller chooses |
1115	| stale price | old price |
1116	| sanity check | check |
1117	| happy path | the normal path |
1118	| atomically | in one transaction |
1119	| trustlessly / permissionlessly | (delete, or name who may call) |
1120	| non-zero | more than zero |
1121	| cascading failures | (name each failure) |
1122	| exponentially / trivially / catastrophically | (delete) |
1123	| edge case | (name the input or the state) |
1124	
1125	## Technical names stay
1126	
1127	STE-100 keeps technical names, and a security report cannot work without them:
1128	`reentrancy`, `slippage`, `oracle`, `revert`, `overflow`, `rounding`, `allowance`, `nonce`,
1129	`slot`, `delegatecall`. Use them. They are precise and the reader who fixes Solidity knows
1130	them.
1131	
1132	The rule is what goes **around** them: name the mechanism once with its technical name, then
1133	say in plain words what the attacker gets.
1134	
1135	- No: `Classic CEI violation enabling reentrant drainage of the vault.`
1136	- Yes: `The function sends ETH before it lowers the balance, so a reentrant call withdraws
1137	  the same deposit twice.`
