988	# Report language — Simplified Technical English
989	
990	Every sentence a human reads in the report obeys this file. The rules are ASD-STE100
991	(Simplified Technical English), reduced to the part an audit finding needs.
992	
993	## Why
994	
995	A report is read by the developer who must fix the code, often at speed, often not in their
996	first language. A sentence they must decode twice costs the fix, and the bug stays in the
997	code. The scan is only as good as the sentence that delivers it.
998	
999	Short words do not make a finding less rigorous. The proof is the diff in the **Fix** block
1000	and the location on the meta line. The sentence exists to say what breaks and who breaks it.
1001	
1002	## What this covers
1003	
1004	Four pieces of text, and no others:
1005	
1006	- the finding **title**
1007	- the **Description** sentence
1008	- the **Lead** description
1009	- the **code smells** list on a Lead
1010	
1011	## What it must never touch
1012	
1013	These are data, not prose. Rewriting one of them for readability breaks the scan:
1014	
1015	- **Solidity identifiers** — contract, function, variable and event names. They are printed as
1016	  the source spells them. `withdrawToNativeChain` is never softened to `withdraw to chain`.
1017	- **The diff inside a Fix block.** It is code. It is pasted verbatim from the agent that wrote
1018	  it, and `judging.md` and `dedup-and-assembly.md` both forbid paraphrase there.
1019	- **The bug-class label** (`zero-abort-address`). It is the third segment of a memory key.
1020	  The same bug under a plainer word is a second ledger record, so memory remembers it twice
1021	  and recognises it never. Labels are chosen by the rules in `dedup-and-assembly.md` step 1,
1022	  never by this file.
1023	- **Fixed strings the assembler prints** — the banner, the Scope table headers, the italic
1024	  lines under Leads and "Known from earlier scans", the disclaimer. They are in
1025	  `assemble.sh` and they are already written.
1026	
1027	## The rules
1028	
1029	1. **One sentence, one idea.** A Description is one sentence. A Lead description is one or
1030	   two. Never three.
1031	2. **Twenty-five words is the ceiling.** Count them. Over the ceiling means two ideas in one
1032	   sentence — cut it or split it.
1033	3. **Active voice, and name the actor.** Who does this? Write that word first.
1034	   - No: `Funds can be drained due to a missing check.`
1035	   - Yes: `Any caller can take all the tokens, because the function checks no caller.`
1036	4. **Simple present tense.** The code does this today. Not `would be able to`, not
1037	   `could potentially`. If the path is uncertain it is a Lead, and the Lead says which step
1038	   is unproven.
1039	5. **No `-ing` clauses.** They hide the actor and the order of events. Use `that`, `so`, or a
1040	   second sentence.
1041	   - No: `Missing access control allowing anyone to drain the vault.`
1042	   - Yes: `The function has no access control, so any caller can take all the tokens.`
1043	6. **Keep the articles.** `the`, `a`, `an`. Telegraphic style — `Function reverts on zero
1044	   amount` — is not shorter to read, only shorter to type.
1045	7. **Three nouns in a row is the limit.** Break a longer stack with `of`, `for` or `in`.
1046	   - No: `cross-chain message replay protection gap`
1047	   - Yes: `the contract does not stop a replay of a cross-chain message`
1048	8. **One word for one thing, every time.** Pick `token`, or pick `asset`, and use that word
1049	   in the title, the Description and the fix. A synonym reads as a second thing.
1050	9. **No metaphor, no idiom, no slang.** See the table below. A metaphor is a word the reader
1051	   must translate before they can act.
1052	10. **Say what happens, not how bad it is.** `catastrophic`, `critical`, `severe` and
1053	    `trivially` carry no information — the confidence number and the Fix block carry it. Write
1054	    the effect: `the caller keeps the tokens and the pool keeps the debt`.
1055	11. **Positive statements. No double negatives.** `The check is absent` beats `the check is
1056	    not present`.
1057	12. **Event order is sentence order.** Cause, then effect. `The price comes from the pool
1058	    balance, so an attacker who moves the balance moves the price.`
1059	13. **A title is one clause, twelve words or fewer,** present tense, active, and it names the
1060	    defect or its effect. `Swap trusts a spot price as an oracle.`
1061	    Not `Oracle manipulation vulnerability in swap functionality`.
1062	
1063	## Words to replace
1064	
1065	Left column: the wording to keep out of the report. Right column: what the report prints.
1066	
1067	| Do not write | Write |
1068	| --- | --- |
1069	| burns the funds | sends the tokens to an address that cannot spend them |
1070	| bricks the contract | makes the function fail for every caller, forever |
1071	| rug / rugpull | the owner takes the user deposits |
1072	| griefing | an attacker makes the function fail for other users |
1073	| footgun | (delete the word and name the defect) |
1074	| silently swallows the error | ignores the error |
1075	| DoS / denial of service | blocks every caller |
1076	| attack surface / attack vector | path |
1077	| malicious actor / bad actor | an attacker |
1078	| leverages / utilizes | uses |
1079	| is able to | can |
1080	| in order to | to |
1081	| arbitrary value | any value the caller chooses |
1082	| stale price | old price |
1083	| sanity check | check |
1084	| happy path | the normal path |
1085	| atomically | in one transaction |
1086	| trustlessly / permissionlessly | (delete, or name who may call) |
1087	| non-zero | more than zero |
